just to give you some flavor of the kind  of vaccines that  potentially may come through over the  next  year or so there are the messenger rna  vaccines  that was the subject of the pfizer  announcement on monday  these vaccines essentially send a coded  message to the immune system  to tell it to make covid antibodies  there are the adenoviral vector vaccines  these insert the covid spike  protein that's the key antigen the key  protein which  allows humans to recognize  the virus and allows the virus to bind  with with the human cell  those are inserted into a harmless or  weakened common cold virus and  that again stimulates antibody  production  then there are the protein adjuvants  protein adjuvanted vaccines these are  slightly older technology  and they are effectively pieces of the  covid virus protein  broken up and when given with an  adjuvant  they stimulate antibody production and  then finally  there is the option of a whole virus  approach  which is where you take the virus and  you kill it  and then it is safely injectable and it  stimulates antibody production  so there are many different approaches  many different manufacturers  and we're just at the beginning of this  journey and it is that  complexity of what we hope will have  soon  versus what we hope will have within  nine to twelve months that makes the job  of the joint committee on vaccination  and immunisation  very challenging indeed but a terribly  important job  in terms of then determining after  authorization  who should receive the vaccine and  professor lim  is here to explain to you some more  about that  thank you very much as jonathan says the  joint committee on vaccination and  immunization  jcvi is an independent expert committee  and we're constituted to advise the  secretary of state  on the provision of vaccination and  immunization  during this pandemic we've been meeting  weekly recently  to appraise ourselves of all the  available  scientific information that is relevant  and  to be able to make the best possible  recommendation in the most timely  manner this is all on an independent  basis i just want to describe  the current interim recommendation from  jcvi  regarding the priority groups for  vaccination when a corporate vaccine  becomes available  the current phase that we would suggest  or recommend  is a phase where we prioritize the most  vulnerable individuals in society  specifically people who are most likely  to die from severe covet 19 infection  and we take into account the important  role of the nhs  and social care as well a further  consideration in our prioritization  is that the program needs to be  recognizable it needs to be  understandable to everybody  and importantly it needs to be  deliverable  we've reviewed data on how the pandemic  is progressing in the uk  the risk factors that are associated  with severe disease from corporate 19  and mathematical models that have looked  at  different vaccination strategies and how  those might work  i'll just come on to the priority groups  as they are defined at the moment  and i just want to underline that these  are interim  current recommendations and they are may  change  as we get more information at the very  top of our priority list are care home  residents  and people who work in care homes  this reflects the devastating impact of  corbett 19  in our care homes as we all know  following on from care home residents we  will prioritize  older individuals going down age bands  down from 80 plus year olds to  60 plus year olds and the reason for  this is because  age is by far the strongest risk factor  associated  with severe coveting disease  following on from those who are age 65  and above  we will then prioritize individuals  particularly adults i should say  who have an underlying health condition  that puts them at risk of corvette 19  disease  falling on from there we will keep going  down the age bands  to individuals who are 50 years and  above  that describes phase one of the program  and phase one as i say is aimed at  protecting the most vulnerable  if phase one is completed then we will  have  protected hopefully over 99 percent  of those individuals who are at risk of  dying from covet 19.  we have not decided yet on who else  should be vaccinated  beyond phase one that's not to say that  they shouldn't be vaccinated but simply  that we haven't decided yet on  prioritization  and that's because we need more  information hopefully  on the vaccines who they are good for  and whether they protect against  transmission or infection
 

